adjacentT -- SEQ 468 (Tony, 2026-10-29): adjacent operands on the new road (Option C).

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each: everything above this file's `stop();`,
then the row on the OLD road (the statement itself), or on the NEW road (a print row through PRINt and testOP; an
expression row through EXPRESSIOn and testOP), then a readback, then `AJ NEXT` (the next statement runs) and a sentinel.

On the old road `print qa qb:;` prints 2 3: one ExpressioN takes both names, interpretXPaccum makes the value a C++ list
(isLIST) and appendGroup walks it (SEQ 465). The new road carries no meaning in C++ flags, so the list is known by its
TAG, ADJACENt: EXPRESSIOn's arm hands a run of adjacent operands up as an ADJACENt list, and PRINt's walk adds its members
as separate print items. A list meeting ANY operator, on either side, call and subscript included, refuses by name in
EXPRESSIOn's kant action -- parked until list operators are ruled (SEQ 467), not forbidden.

ROWS -- id | kind | statement | readback (E rows). P = a print statement, E = an expression.
AJ-0 controls a single operand, AJ-0b an ordinary operator. AJ-3: in a print `-` is a SHORTCUt, on both roads, so it never
reaches EXPRESSIOn as a prefix; AJ-4 asks the prefix question with `!`, which is not a shortcut (R3: the arm adds the
prefix-wrapped operand).

AJ-0|P|print qa:;
AJ-0b|P|print qa + qb:;
AJ-1|P|print qa qb:;
AJ-2|P|print qa qb qc:;
AJ-3|P|print - qa qb:;
AJ-4|P|print ! qa qb:;
AJ-R1|E|holdB +% qa qb qc|cerr "AJ val " holdB.listLengtH:;
